The bar chart shows the sampling distribution of $\bar{X}$ for samples of size $4$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.72]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,11.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(13.5,0);
\foreach \y/\lbl in {2/0.1,4/0.2,6/0.3,8/0.4,10/0.5} {\draw (-0.2,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\y) {$\lbl$};}
\node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,0) {$0$};
\draw (0.8,0) rectangle (2.3,1);
\draw (3.1,0) rectangle (4.6,4);
\draw (5.4,0) rectangle (6.9,10);
\draw (7.7,0) rectangle (9.2,4);
\draw (10.0,0) rectangle (11.5,1);
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (1.55,0) {$4$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (3.85,0) {$5$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (6.15,0) {$6$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (8.45,0) {$7$};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (10.75,0) {$8$};
\node[rotate=90] at (-1.5,5) {$P(\bar{X}=\bar{x})$};
\node[below] at (6.15,-1.0) {$\bar{x}$};
\node at (6.15,11.1) {Distribution of $\bar{X}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Find $P(\bar{X}\le 5)$.
